"In the noble copies of the Quran (mushafs), there are two kinds of Basmalah. The first is the Basmalah written at the beginning of the surahs, independent of the surah itself. The second is the Basmalah in the 30th verse of Surah an-Naml. There is no doubt that this Basmalah is a verse from the Quran, and this is certain by unanimous consensus of the scholars. However, whether the Basmalah at the beginning of surahs is part of the Quran is a disputed matter in the sciences of tafsir and usul (principles of Islamic jurisprudence). It is particularly related to the topics of faith, prayer (salah), and recitation (qira'ah).\n\nScholars such as Sa'id ibn Jubayr, al-Zuhri, 'Ata', and Ibn al-Mubarak stated that the Basmalah at the beginning of surahs is a verse of that surah. Imam al-Shafi'i and his students hold this view. According to them, the first of the seven verses of al-Fatihah is the Basmalah, and it is recited aloud in prayer. Imam Malik, on the other hand, relying on the tradition of the people of Medina, argues that the Basmalah at the beginning of surahs is not part of the Quran, but was written to separate the surahs and to seek blessing (tabarruk). For this reason, the Malikis do not recite the Basmalah in prayer. The Hanafis, however, accept that the Basmalah is an independent verse in its own right, but not an integral part of the surahs. Therefore, it was deemed appropriate to recite it silently in prayer.\n\nThe Basmalah ostensibly consists of four words: \"Bismillahir-Rahmanir-Rahim\". In reality, however, it consists of seven words. The preposition \"bi\" (with/by) is attached to an omitted (elliptical) verb, and this verb carries a meaning such as \"I begin\". The word \"ism\" (name) is the sign that allows a thing to be brought to mind. \"Allah\", on the other hand, is the proper name of the true Deity. The attributes \"al-Rahman\" and \"al-Rahim\" express the mercy of Allah. It is Sunnah to recite the Basmalah at the beginning of every important undertaking.\n\nThe supreme name \"Allah\" is the proper name of the Necessary Being whose existence is essential. This name encompasses within itself all attributes of perfection. Without accepting the existence and oneness of Allah, it is impossible to understand the universe. This name is the foundation of all sciences. Within human beings there is a latent feeling concerning Allah, and this feeling is stronger than all other emotions. The Quran was revealed to remind of and awaken this feeling.\n\nThe name \"Allah\" is different from generic words such as \"God\" (deity/god) or \"Ilah\". \"God\" is a common noun and can also be used for false deities. \"Allah\", on the other hand, is used only for the true Deity and has no plural form. For this reason, the name \"Allah\" cannot be fully translated into other languages. Sulayman Çelebi used the name \"Allah\" in his Mawlid and did not say \"God\". Similarly, the French word \"dieu\" corresponds to \"Ilah\", not \"Allah\".\n\nIt is known that the name Allah is Arabic and has been in use since the time of Ishmael (peace be upon him). In the Quran, too, it is recounted that when the polytheists were asked, \"Who created the heavens and the earth?\" they replied, \"Allah.\" Therefore, it is certain that this name belongs to the Arabic language. Although some claim that it was borrowed from Hebrew or Syriac, it cannot be proven that these languages antedate Arabic. Furthermore, the fact that words such as Israel and Gabriel (Jibril) are of foreign origin does not indicate that the word Allah is as well.\n\nFrom a grammatical perspective, it has been debated whether the prefix \"al-\" at the beginning of the word Allah can be separated or not. According to one view, \"al-\" is an inseparable part of the word; the fact that the hamzah is not elided in the vocative case proves this. According to the other view, \"al-\" is the definite article, and the word is derived from \"lah\" or \"ilah\". The Kufan grammarians argued that it was formed by the contraction of \"al-ilah\"; scholars such as al-Zamakhshari and al-Baydawi adopted this view.\n\nOther scholars, however, have stated that the word Allah is underived (murtajal) and is an inherently proper noun. According to Abu Hayyan and Imam al-Razi, the word has been from the very beginning an exclusive name belonging solely to Allah. The fact that the hamzah is not elided in the vocative case and that it is never applied to any other being is the most significant evidence of this. According to this view, the attribute of divinity is not derived from the name; rather, the name encompasses the attribute.\n\nThe name al-Rahman is likewise an attributive name unique solely to Allah. It means \"the All-Merciful,\" yet this attribute has never been used for any other being. Grammatically, it is a predominant adjective (sifah ghalibah); it does not govern like a verb, and it is used in annexation (idafah). Al-Rahman cannot be translated; it can only be interpreted as \"the Exceedingly Merciful.\" Al-Rahim, on the other hand, is an adjective and can also be used for entities other than Allah.\n\nThere is an important difference between al-Rahman and al-Rahim: the mercy of al-Rahman is general and necessary; it encompasses every being and is not contingent upon any condition. The mercy of al-Rahim, however, is specific and conditional; it belongs exclusively to the believers. That is why it has been said, \"The Rahman of this world, the Rahim of the Hereafter.\" Were it not for the mercy of al-Rahman, created beings could not have been created; and were it not for the mercy of al-Rahim, effort and progress would not be possible.\n\nThe attributes al-Rahman and al-Rahim are distinct from each other; they are not repeated merely for emphasis. Al-Rahman is an eternal attribute of Allah and pertains to willing goodness at the inception; al-Rahim, on the other hand, is an active attribute pertaining to conferring goodness at the consummation. The mercy of al-Rahman is general, encompassing every created thing; the mercy of al-Rahim is more specific, an actual outpouring of grace. This distinction demonstrates that Allah's mercy is manifold, and there is no reason for despair for the believer.\n\nThe Basmalah ostensibly consists of a genitive construct (idafah) and two descriptive phrases (adjectival constructs), preceded by the preposition \"ba\". In reality, it is an abbreviated form of a hidden verbal sentence. The letter \"ba\" connects the action that the person pronouncing the Basmalah is about to perform (such as reading, writing, or eating) to the name of Allah. This hidden verb and subject are understood from the context of the discourse, and just as in Arabic, there are similar usages in Turkish as well.\n\nTranslating the Basmalah into Turkish is quite difficult. Various forms have been attempted, yet problems exist in each of them. Expressions such as \"In the name of Rahman, Rahim Allah\" either lead to a loss of meaning or arouse suspicions of the Trinity (tathlith). Therefore, it is most appropriate to pronounce the Basmalah as it is and reflect upon its meaning through tafsirs. It is necessary to remain faithful to the original and avoid defective translations.\n\nThe most important element in the Basmalah is the initial letter \"ba\". By means of this letter, a person connects the deed they are about to perform to the name of Allah. The \"ba\" conveys the meaning of either taking refuge and accompaniment, or seeking assistance (isti'anah). According to Sheikh Muhammad Abduh, the Basmalah signifies performing an act in the name of Allah, just as a judge delivers a verdict in the name of the sovereign. Although this interpretation holds true for specific offices, it cannot be applied to general usage.\n\nThe Basmalah is obligatory (fard) when writing the Quran (except for Surah at-Tawbah); it is likewise obligatory when slaughtering animals and hunting. In prayer, reciting it silently is Sunnah according to the Hanafis, and obligatory (fard) according to the Shafi'is. Beginning important affairs with the Basmalah is either recommended (mandub) or obligatory (wajib), depending on the significance of the act. In the hadiths, it is stated: \"The Basmalah is the key to every book,\" and \"Any important matter that does not begin with the Basmalah remains severed of blessing.\"\n\nIn short, the Basmalah is a unique maxim that summarizes the oneness of Allah, His mercy, and the relationship of servitude ('ubudiyyah). It is positioned at the beginning of every surah of the Quran and has become an established tradition for the entire Islamic Ummah. The Basmalah is the key to everything and holds infinite wisdom within it. The harmony and order among the verses of the Quran likewise demonstrate the miraculous nature (i'jaz) of this divine Word.\n\nHuman reason has been able to grasp the laws of attraction in the universe only in their material dimension. Yet in the realm of existence, there are also chemical, biological, spiritual, and social forces of attraction. The Quran presents a universal law of attraction that unites these different forces of attraction and encompasses every kind of existential relationship. In this law, we shall contemplate the absolute attraction of the creative Power, the bond between Allah and existence, and especially its manifestations in human relations.\n\nThe composition (nazm) of the Quran is a realm where scientific order and aesthetic order embrace each other. Its logical order is sounder than any compilation produced by science and art, and its literary beauties are more colorful and varied. The harmony between the clear verses (muhkamat) and the allegorical verses (mutashabihat) can only be seen in creation itself. As stated in Surah an-Nisa, verse 82, many contradictions should have been found in the Quran, yet none were found.\n\nThe order among the verses of the Quran is more distinct and profound than the order of celestial bodies. This order is both zahir (apparent) and batin (hidden). Its being apparent stems from its perfection, and its being hidden stems from its infinity. A poet expressed this state by saying: 'The virgin thoughts of the universe were shattered into pieces, yet the meanings of the Quran have still remained within the veil of protection.'\n\nAbove all, in order to be purified from heedlessness and whisperings of doubt, we must cling to the Basmalah by saying, 'I seek refuge in Allah from the accursed Satan.' Surah al-Fatihah begins with praise (hamd) to Allah and contains the following verses: Praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds, the Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful, and Sovereign of the Day of Recompense. You alone we worship, and from You alone we seek help. Guide us to the straight path—the path of those upon whom You have bestowed favor, not of those who have evoked anger or of those who are astray.\n\nBetween the Basmalah and al-Fatihah, there are strong ties in terms of antecedent context (sibaq) and the flow of discourse (siyaq). The Basmalah consists of seven words: four independent words and three names of Allah. Al-Fatihah, on the other hand, consists of seven verses, four fundamental statements, and three pause points. In response to the appeal of mercy in the Basmalah, al-Fatihah is an expression of gratitude. Al-Fatihah is the explanation of the Basmalah and the manifestation of its meanings.\n\nAccording to what is stated in a Hadith Qudsi, Surah al-Fatihah has been divided equally between Allah and the servant. The first three verses belong to Allah, and the last three verses belong to the servant; the verse in the middle, 'You alone we worship, and from You alone we seek help,' is between the two. This balance reflects justice, which is the foundation of the cosmic order. At the same time, it points to a social reality such as the consensus (ijma') of the Ummah being a legal proof.\n\nHamd is praise situated between madih (general commendation) and shukr (gratitude), rendered in response to a voluntary good. Madih can also be made to inanimate beings, whereas hamd is directed only toward an entity possessing will and following an act of goodness. While shukr is in return for a blessing that has reached one, hamd can also be offered for a good that has not yet materialized. Hamd and shukr are always grounded in truth and are moral; madih, however, often remains mere falsehood. The Prophet commanded hamd and forbade excessive praise.\n\nWhen hamd and shukr are compared, the aspect of respect and valuation in hamd weighs heavier. For hamd is praise rendered even if the bestowed blessing has not reached the person; in this respect, it is more sincere and directed toward the truth. Furthermore, hamd encompasses virtues such as sharing the joy of those who have attained blessings and regarding that which has not yet arrived as having arrived. For this reason, the most perfect form of shukr is hamd offered with the tongue. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'Hamd is the head of gratitude. Whoever does not praise Allah has not thanked Him.'\n\nHamd encompasses all verbal praises, remembrances (dhikr), and expressions of respect made with the tongue. When one says 'Praise be to Allah (praise be to Allah)', the feeling of happiness and bliss experienced in the face of a good deed or bestowal worthy of all these respects is proclaimed. This means both praising the author of that goodness and rejoicing in that happiness. Hamd is a moral virtue, like proclaiming the blessing and eradicating ingratitude. From the standpoint of humanity, happiness can be summarized in two words: tana'um (being in blessing and abundance) and in'am (bestowing blessings). Happiness lies not in prosperity itself, but in its being felt and appreciated. Hamd is precisely the verbal expression of this feeling.\n\nThe stations of hamd are respectively as follows: Hamidiyyah (praising), Mahmudiyyah (being praiseworthy), Hamidiyyah and Mahmudiyyah, Mahmudiyyah and Hamidiyyah. At the highest station, one of the divine manifestations belonging to Allah is seen. At this station, Allah's beautiful names such as al-Hamid and al-Mahmud manifest. Furthermore, Ahmad and Muhammad, among the blessed names of the Prophet (peace be upon him), also point to this station. Maqam al-Mahmud (the Praiseworthy Station) is the highest station of intercession promised to the Prophet. At this station, 'Liwa al-Hamd' (the Banner of Praise) will be handed to him, and his Ummah will gather under this banner. The conclusion of the prayers of the people of Paradise will also be: 'Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds.'\n\nAl-Fatihah, beginning with 'Alhamdulillah', teaches us these happinesses and goals of maturity. The letter 'lam' in the phrase 'Alhamdulillah' carries the meanings of belonging, entitlement, and ownership. The sentence means: 'The reality of praise, in all its varieties and degrees, is exclusive to Allah, is Allah's right, and is Allah's possession.' This nominal sentence expresses continuous and perpetual praise. There is no other nominal sentence in al-Fatihah; the other sentences are verbal sentences and explain this nominal sentence.\n\n'Rabb al-Alamin' is an attribute of Allah. Because 'Alamin' is a plural designating beings endowed with intellect, it means 'the Lord of all realms, and especially the realms of intelligent beings.' This expression points to the wisdom behind the revelation of the Quran. The word 'Rabb' derives from the verbal root meaning to nurture and rear (tarbiyah) and is a name given to the one who nurtures. It expresses hyperbolic emphasis; that is, it signifies a flawless nurturer possessing everything necessary for upbringing. It also conveys the meanings of owner and master. However, 'Rabb' cannot be used alone for anyone other than Allah.\n\nThe tarbiyah (nurturing) of the Rabb is to bring a thing step by step to maturity. Throughout the worlds, the laws of nurture and maturation are visible at every moment. This state demonstrates Allah's infinite power. When the Lord of the worlds is mentioned, one contemplates the law of nurture in the universe. Some philosophers failed to see this law, claiming that the universe was created all at once or had existed from eternity. Yet observation and intellect demonstrate that everything comes into being gradually, develops, and then ceases to be. The formation of the human being is also an example of this process.\n\nCreation, nurture, and selection in the universe constitute a divine order and are the work of Allah's absolute perfection. In recent years, science and philosophy have accepted the existence of the law of evolution/development (takamul) in nature. However, some do not attribute this evolution to Allah, but ascribe it to a blind and unconscious nature. Yet science is based on the law of causality. The principle that 'nothing comes from nothing' states that every originated thing has a cause. Naturalism contradicts this law. There is harmony and proportionality between cause and effect. For this reason, scientists who accept evolution in nature are compelled to conceive of Allah in His infinite perfection.\n\nFor example, a grain of wheat falls into the soil and yields a hundred grains. This is an increase in quantity. If this increase is attributed solely to the nature of the first grain, it becomes a logical fallacy like '1=100'. Yet at the culmination of every development, there is a maturity that arrives from outside. This is development attained through nurture (tarbiyah). The rule 'the complete does not arise from the deficient; the deficient arises from the complete' applies. Therefore, science and philosophy accept the existence and perfection of Allah. Naturalists, contradicting their own theories, still do not abandon the notion of nurture, whereas without Allah's nurture, these claims remain groundless.\n\nThe word ''alam' (world/realm) is a noun of instrument meaning an instrument of knowledge, and its plural form, ''alamin', is a plural used for rational beings. Its usage with the definite article 'lam' as 'al-'alamin' encompasses the entire universe and all of its parts. Employing a plural designating rational beings within this generality demonstrates that they are held superior and points to the significance of rational beings in understanding the Lord of the worlds.\n\nEvery entity in the universe is a sign pointing to the existence and Lordship (Rububiyyah) of Allah. This indication begins with a brief sign at first glance, then deepens through detailed reflection. The connection between human perceptions and physical things necessitates the existence of Allah. Even while perceiving our own existence, we are compelled to acknowledge Allah, Who encompasses us and the external world; otherwise, we cannot verify any reality.\n\nThe attributes of Rahman and Rahim express the mercy of Allah. Allah's mercy is not an emotional impression like human compassion, but consists of essential (dhati) and active (fi'li) attributes. Rahman indicates the will to create and bestow existence, whereas Rahim signifies the act of bestowing blessings and doing good. These two attributes denote that Allah's mercy is both all-encompassing and specific.\n\nThe mercy of the Rahman reaches all beings equally as they emerge from non-existence into existence. The mercy of the Rahim, on the other hand, is directed toward beings endowed with free will, administering reward and punishment according to their choices. This is a requirement of Allah's justice and wisdom. While associating partners with Allah (shirk) and rebellion render the continuation of existence impossible, monotheism (tawhid) preserves the balance of mercy.\n\nWhile acting with partial free will (iradah juz'iyyah), man should not fall into the error of regarding this will as absolute. Allah is both the First and the Last, the Manifest and the Hidden. In the present time, just as in the past, everything is in His hand of power. Man's aspect of helplessness bears witness to Allah's power, while his aspect of choice bears witness to His will. This balance condemns man neither to determinism (jabr) nor to absolute freedom; rather, through Allah's mercy, it holds responsibility and freedom together.\n\nIn this section, while explaining the expression \"Maliki yawmi-d-din\", Elmalılı emphasizes that man is not in the position of giving reward and punishment, but of receiving them. It is stated that Allah is the true owner of all dominion, and that temporary authorities in this world are merely borrowed. His sovereignty is an absolute ownership encompassing the heavens, the earths, and all realms.\n\nThe concept of \"Yawmi-d-din\" is examined: it is stated that the word \"din\" in Arabic carries meanings such as retribution, reckoning, judgment, obedience, and sacred law (sharia), and that it does not directly mean the Day of Resurrection. It is noted that, in addition to being understood here as the day of retribution, it is also interpreted in the familiar sense of religion. What is meant is the final day when every deed will be recompensed, that is, the Day of the Hereafter. It is explained that the word \"day\" denotes absolute time, and that the days of the Hereafter are expressed with different measures compared to worldly days.\n\nIt is explained that \"yawmi-d-din\" points to the Day of Resurrection, but is not the resurrection itself. It is emphasized that the Day of Resurrection encompasses stages such as resurrection, gathering (hashr), reckoning, the scale (mizan), and the traverse (sirat), and that the phrase \"yawmi-d-din\" reinforces encouragement and warning by highlighting the day of retribution, which is the final of these stages. For this reason, it is stated that the expression \"the Day of Din\" should not be translated simply as \"the Day of Resurrection\" or \"the Day of Reckoning,\" but rather that it conveys a stronger meaning.\n\nThe definition of religion (din) is provided: Religion is a divine system that guides rational beings toward goodness by their own free will. It is explained that this definition encompasses both true religion and false religions. The prerequisite of religion is intellect and free will, whereas religiosity requires knowledge and love. The difference between religion and religiosity is like the difference between divine law and human effort. The fruit of religion consists of deeds that are inherently good; goodness is done because it is good in the sight of Allah.\n\nThe distinguishing characteristic of the true religion is that it is based on voluntary obedience, not compulsion. There is no compulsion in religion; man must choose goodness through his free will. Doing good solely for self-interest is not genuine religiosity; what is essential is to do good for the sake of Allah's pleasure. The law of Allah is known through revelation and prophethood; intellect and transmitted tradition work together at this point. In Islam, the authoritative sources are the Book, the Sunnah, consensus (ijma), and analogical reasoning (qiyas).\n\nFinally, it is stated that the true religion will be verified through the fulfillment of its promises, and that Islamic history bears witness to this. It is expressed that today's utilitarian mentality diminishes the influence of the true religion, yet the reality of the Day of Din serves as a warning against such trends. Elmalılı interprets the expression \"Maliki yawmi-d-din\" both in the sense of the day of retribution and the day of religion in the well-known sense, suggesting that it be translated as \"Master of the Day of Din.\" Furthermore, the recitation variants are addressed, explaining the subtle nuance between the readings \"Malik\" and \"Malik\".\n\nOwnership (malikiyyah) is the private right of disposal that a person possesses over wealth and property for their own benefit. Authority, on the other hand, is the power of sovereignty. In reality, both belong to Allah, the Lord of the worlds. The property and authority possessed by human beings, however, are temporary and held in trust. Nonetheless, in this world, human selfishness and pride rely on this temporary property and authority. The happiness of life depends on the provision of private property and social order. Neither extreme socialism nor extreme individualism is beneficial for humanity. Both must be in balance.\n\nThe phrase \"maliki yawmi-d-din\" in the verse conveys that on that day no one will be able to help anyone else and that true dominion belongs to Allah alone. This warning is terrifying for those who rely on their temporary possessions in this world. However, this verse does not impart despair; for on that day, by the mercy of Allah, believers will receive their share of bliss, while disbelievers will be deprived. The two recitations, \"Malik\" and \"malik\", emphasize this warning separately in terms of individual ownership and social ownership.\n\nThe first three verses of al-Fatihah constitute a proposition consisting of one premise and five proofs. Praise belongs exclusively to Allah, because He is worthy of it by virtue of His Essence and His attributes. He deserves it by His Essence because He is Allah. He deserves it by His attributes because He is the Lord of the worlds, the Rahman, the Rahim, and the Master of the Day of Din. Addressing mankind, these verses remind them of Allah's power and mercy, directing them to worship Him alone.\n\nThe expression \"Iyyaka na'budu\" declares that servitude and worship must be directed to Allah alone. Worship is a specific act of obedience expressing drawing near to Allah based on intention. Every act of worship is a drawing near, but not every drawing near is an act of worship. Deeds performed without intention are not considered acts of worship. For this reason, intention is a prerequisite in acts of worship such as prayer, fasting, and zakat. The essence of worship is complete submission and humility before Allah.\n\nThe human soul experiences a continuous ebb and flow between hope and fear. When hope is present, activity increases; when fear is present, either transgression or despair arises. When man realizes his helplessness, he turns to Allah, the sole source of all hopes and fears. This turning is indeed the beginning of the feeling of worship. However, humans often fall into polytheism (shirk) by attaching this sentiment to transient things. True worship is devotion rendered exclusively to Allah, bringing peace to the heart.\n\nWhen the Prophet (peace be upon him) passed away, Umar denied the event, but Abu Bakr calmed the Companions by reciting verse 3/144 of Surah Al 'Imran. He said, \"If you worship Muhammad, he has died; but if you worship Allah, He never dies.\" This event demonstrates how wrong it is for people to attach themselves to transient beings. A believer is like a fresh crop; as the wind blows, it bends, but it rises again. A disbeliever is like a pine tree; once it is brought down, it cannot rise again.\n\nWorship requires hope and fear to unite in a single source. A person finds peace when directing all their fears and hopes toward Allah. The pride of the ego may object, thinking that worship is humiliation. In truth, real elevation is possible only through submitting to Allah. Arrogance and pride are an illness for which worship is the remedy. The servant must be able to say, \"I did not exist, Allah brought me into being; I would sacrifice everything in His path.\" This is the very submission taught by the Fatihah.\n\nThe expression \"Iyyaka nasta'in\" emphasizes that help must be sought from Allah alone. The servant knows that they cannot worship by their own power and asks for help from Allah. This seeking of help is essential for the continuation of worship. By combining servitude and the plea for help with these two sentences, the Fatihah forms the foundation of a Muslim's life.\n\nIn the statement \"You alone we worship and from You alone we ask for help,\" \"asking for help\" (nasta'in) can be understood in three ways: asking for help in a general sense, asking for help in every matter, or asking for help in a specific matter based on a particular contextual indication. Although the third possibility might come to mind due to the context of worship, the statement's orientation toward tawhid by means of abbreviation and specification necessitates asking for general help. Help is of two kinds: necessary help (things without which the action cannot occur, such as power and intellect) and unnecessary help (things that facilitate the task). In the science of jurisprudence (usul), this division is known by the names qudrah mumakkinah (enabling power) and qudrah muyassirah (facilitating power).\n\nThis verse shows us that all help belongs to Allah, but the willing is ours. This willing is termed partial free will (juz'i iradah) or acquisition (kasb). The actions a person performs with their own will express the sum of the connection between a desirer (the human being) and a Creator (Allah). Creation depends not only on the immediate proximate cause, but on the coming together of all causes. For example, like the operation of a machine: the machine is the locus of the work, the machinist is the one who wills, and the Creator is Allah. These three levels are an evident reality. One should neither presume man to be a soulless machine like the Jabriyyah, nor consider man a creator like Allah.\n\nThe expression \"You alone we worship and from You alone we ask for help\" bears the character of a covenant (ahd) between Allah and His servants. This covenant possesses a collective rather than an individual dimension; for the plural form is used as \"we worship\" and \"we ask for help.\" This points to the virtue of congregational worship and aims at the formation of a collective spirit. This collective spirit develops through directing love and fear toward Allah alone, transforming into a universal feeling of brotherhood. Indeed, Islam is this great collective spirit itself.\n\nThe first person to realize this covenant in the fullest sense was the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him). In his solitude in the Cave of Hira, he possessed a collective conscience that represented all of humanity, and he received this covenant from Allah and conveyed it to his Ummah. By adhering to his Sunnah, the Islamic community was able to truly utter this covenant of servitude and established an exemplary state in the world. If one were to say \"I worship You alone\" while reciting the Fatihah in prayer, the prayer would be invalidated; because Allah wills to make a covenant with a collective conscience, not an individual one.\n\nThis verse expresses both the oneness of Allah (tawhid) and the unity of servitude. The phrase \"You alone we worship\" contains the testimony of tawhid and rejects all forms of polytheism (shirk). Here, there are two types of obligation: individual obligation (fard al-ayn, belonging to each individual) and collective obligation (fard al-kifayah, belonging to the community). However, in this verse, without erasing the personal meaning, each individual uttering this statement on behalf of the community is understood as an individual obligation. In this manner, Islam unites the consciences of both the individual and society at a single point, elevating them to the most perfect rank of tawhid.\n\nIn his commentary on Surah al-Fatihah, Elmalılı Hamdi Yazır addresses the belief of shirk in detail. He divides those who associate partners with Allah into various categories: corporeal partners (such as idols, trees, animals, and humans), simple bodies (the Magians who worship fire), celestial bodies (the Sabians who worship the sun, moon, and stars), and non-corporeal partners (such as spiritual entities, angels, and the belief in two deities). All such beliefs are shirk and contradict the oneness of Allah.\n\nYazır especially criticizes the belief in two deities (Thanawiyyah) and emphasizes its logical inconsistency. He points out that two separate primary principles cannot establish an order, that this would necessitate a third common ground, and that it ultimately leads to the doctrine of the Trinity in Christianity. He states that humanity deviated from the belief in one God and plunged into various forms of shirk, but Islam corrected these deviations with true tawhid. In this context, he states that the address \"ya ayyuha-lladhina amanu\" in Surah al-Fatihah is an evidence rejecting shirk.\n\nYazır then proceeds to the supplication \"ihdina-s-sirata-l-mustaqim\". He emphasizes that this supplication is an explanation of the expression \"nasta'in\" (we ask for help) and that the most important help from Allah is guidance (hidayah). He lists four kinds of guidance: granting material and spiritual faculties, showing the proofs that distinguish truth from falsehood, sending prophets and scriptures, and the unveiling (kashf) and inspiration (ilham) granted specifically to the saints (awliya). He notes that guidance is direction toward goodness, and that its use for evil actions is metaphorical.\n\nYazır analyzes the expression \"sirat al-mustaqim\". He explains that the word \"sirat\" means a busy, broad way, and that \"mustaqim\" means completely straight with no crookedness whatsoever. He states that these two words combine to express the path of truth, the community (millah) of Islam. He touches upon the different recitations (qira'at) of \"sirat\" in Arabic and draws attention to the wisdom behind the word selections in the Quran. In addition, he distinguishes between the physical and spiritual dimensions of the concept of \"path.\"\n\nFinally, Yazır interprets the expression \"sirata-lladhina an'amta 'alayhim\". He states that \"in'am\" means bestowing blessings and that Allah's blessings cannot be counted. He explains that the word \"ni'mah\" (blessing) originally means tasting pleasure, and that blessings are divided into worldly and otherworldly. By briefly explaining the concepts of \"maghdub\" (those who have incurred wrath) and \"dallin\" (those who have gone astray), he emphasizes that the straight path is far removed from these two forms of deviance.\n\nBlessings are divided into two: divinely bestowed (wahbi) and acquired through effort (kasbi). These are also divided into spiritual and corporeal subcategories. Among spiritual blessings are the breathing of the spirit, intellect, and intelligence; corporeal blessings include the body and its organs. Acquired blessings, on the other hand, are things like purifying the soul, knowledge, good character, wealth, and status. Blessings of the Hereafter consist of attaining Allah's pleasure and finding peace in Paradise. However, the end of worldly blessings can sometimes turn into disaster; the true blessing consists of the sound blessings that serve as a means to happiness in the Hereafter.\n\nThere are two views regarding the interpretation of the expression 'An'amta 'alayhim': The first is the meaning 'You bestowed favor upon them,' that is, You made them felicitous, without presupposing any direct object. The second is 'You bestowed that path upon them as a blessing' by assuming an implied direct object (maf'ul). The author of al-Kashshaf preferred the first, while al-Tabari preferred the second. According to Elmalılı, the most correct approach is to understand this expression as 'metonymy for bestowing the straight path as a blessing'; thus, the blessing of the path (sirat) acquires a meaning that encompasses all blessings.\n\nThe blessing of the path is the greatest blessing, because obtaining the path to any blessing ensures continuous access to that blessing. For this reason, praying 'Guide us to the straight path' has been taught in the Fatihah; this is superior to asking for specific blessings. When the straight path is found, all paths of blessings are reached, and blessings become continuous. This is also why the Fatihah is called 'ta'lim al-mas'alah' (instruction on how to supplicate).\n\nThose upon whom favor has been bestowed are the prophets, the steadfast affirmers of truth (siddiqin), the martyrs, and the righteous. Surah an-Nisa 4:69 explains this in the most comprehensive manner. The most perfect example is the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) and his noble Companions. The success and happiness they attained in history have not been witnessed in any other community. For this reason, the expression 'those upon whom You have bestowed favor' specifically refers to the path of the Prophet Muhammad and his Companions.\n\nIt is disputed whether the word 'ghayri' is an adjective (sifah) or an apposition (badal). While al-Kashshaf and Qadi Baydawi state that it can be a badal, Abu al-Su'ud rejects this and maintains that it is a sifah. Elmalılı also adopts Abu al-Su'ud's view: 'Ghayri' is an adjective and indicates that those upon whom favor has been bestowed are those who are far from wrath and misguidance. Thus, true blessing also includes being saved from wrath and misguidance, which is precisely what Islam is.\n\nWrath is Allah's will to punish; misguidance (dalalah) is deviating from the right path. In the Quran, wrath is generally attributed to the Jews, and misguidance to the Christians. Although this encompasses other disbelievers as well, they are mentioned as the most prominent examples. In a hadith narrated by al-Tirmidhi, the Prophet also stated: 'The Jews are those who incurred wrath, and the Christians are the astray.' However, the generality of the ayah must be preserved, and it should not be restricted solely to these two groups.\n\nThe hadith of 'Adi b. Hatim supports this tafsir. According to the narration, the Prophet stated that the Jews are the 'maghdubi 'alayhim' and the Christians are the 'dallin'. This is an important explanation in the exegesis of the Fatihah. Nevertheless, some commentators have stated that it is more appropriate to preserve the general meaning of the ayah and accept the Jews and Christians as illustrative examples.\n\nThere are hadiths stating that the expressions 'maghdubi 'alayhim' and 'dallin' in the Fatihah refer to the Jews and Christians, yet the meaning of the ayah is not limited to them alone. Since these hadiths are not mutawatir (mass-transmitted), restricting the ayah is objectionable. There are two forms of interpretation: In the first, the Jews and Christians are intended merely as textual referents, whereas in the second, this distinction is demonstrated more comprehensively by way of meaning and implication. Elmalılı finds the second interpretation more in keeping with the style of the Quran.\n\nIn the time of the Messenger of Allah, the Jews, out of love for the world and selfishness, had corrupted the Torah and, by acting unjustly toward the prophets, had incurred Allah's wrath. Having lost their political freedom, they were dispersed and involved with secret societies. Although they possessed an illuminating scripture in the past, at that time they had become a community whose interior did not match their exterior, striving to suppress the truth.\n\nThe Christians, on the other hand, were a great empire at that time as the heirs of Rome and masters of Constantinople (Istanbul). Outwardly, they seemed to have attained blessings, but in reality they were in danger. Entrenched in the belief in a Trinity of deities, having halted intellectual progress, leaving the law to the whims of the clergy, and disregarding human rights, this misguidance had made them candidates for decline in this world and punishment in the Hereafter. Allah sent the religion of Islam, free from these perils.\n\nSurah al-Fatihah describes the knowledge of Allah and the creation of the universe at its beginning, the principles of the religion of Islam in its middle, and the straight path in its final three verses. With the expression 'not the path of those who have incurred wrath, nor of those who are astray,' it determines the boundary of the true path. This boundary is the safe and straight path that leads to the blessings of Allah. The surah summarizes this meaning with the 'Basmalah' at its beginning.\n\nThe religion of Islam is a true path established by history and experience. Our Prophet and his Companions attained immense blessings by walking upon this path. Because the Quran and the Sunnah have been preserved without corruption, the reality of Islam has not changed. The decline of contemporary Muslims stems not from the religion itself, but from their failure to practice it properly. As people attempt to adapt the law of the Truth to themselves, distress increases; the solution is to persevere upon the straight path.\n\nSaying 'Amin' at the end of the Fatihah is a Sunnah. 'Amin' means 'accept' and is not part of the Quran. Our Prophet commanded the congregation to say 'amin' when the imam says 'wa la-d-dallin', informing that the angels also say 'amin' and that whoever's utterance coincides with theirs will have their sins forgiven. It is appropriate for both the imam and the congregation to say it silently."